Howdy on Fedora
===============

This package installs Howdy but does NOT enable it. Nothing in /etc/pam.d is
modified on install, upgrade or removal: you decide where face
authentication is used, and your password always keeps working.

The installation section of the upstream README.md does not apply to this
package; follow this file instead.

Face recognition is more convenient than a password, not more secure. A
similar-looking person or a good photo may be enough to unlock your account.


1. Configure the camera
-----------------------

Find your IR camera. Stable paths live in /dev/v4l/by-path/:

    ls -l /dev/v4l/by-path/
    v4l2-ctl --list-devices        # from the v4l-utils package

A camera can expose several nodes; if `sudo howdy test` shows no image or a
colour image, try the next one. Set it and try it:

    sudo howdy set device_path /dev/v4l/by-path/<your-ir-camera>
    sudo howdy test

`sudo howdy config` opens the whole configuration in $EDITOR (nano or vi
by default).


2. Enroll your face
-------------------

    sudo howdy add

Face models are stored in /etc/howdy/models/<user>.dat. They must be
readable by all local users because the KDE Plasma lock screen runs the
check as your own user, not as root.

Howdy writes the model file with the umask of the command that runs it. With
a strict umask such as 077 the file ends up 0600 and the KDE lock screen
cannot read it. After `howdy add`, or after `howdy remove` if the file still
exists, check and fix the permissions:

    ls -l /etc/howdy/models/
    sudo chmod 0644 /etc/howdy/models/$USER.dat


3. Enable Howdy in PAM
----------------------

Only use this recipe for services that ask for a PASSWORD ALONE.

A "sufficient" module that succeeds ends the authentication stack: every
module after it is skipped, including any mandatory second factor. Check
your configuration first:

    authselect current

If it lists with-pam-u2f-2fa (security key required), or the service uses
any other mandatory factor (smart card, OTP, a "required" auth module after
the password), do NOT add pam_howdy.so as below: a recognised face would skip
both the password and the second factor. Combining Howdy with a mandatory
second factor is not covered by this package.

Add this line as the FIRST "auth" line of each PAM service where you want
face authentication (after the pam_selinux_permit.so line, if there is one):

    auth        sufficient    pam_howdy.so

"sufficient" means a recognised face logs you in, and anything else (no
face, no camera, timeout, error) falls through to the normal password
prompt.

Keep a root shell open (`sudo -i`) while editing, and test from a NEW
terminal before closing it, so a typo cannot lock you out of sudo.

Do not edit system-auth or password-auth: authselect owns them and will
overwrite your changes. Edit only the per-service files below.

  Where                              File
  ---------------------------------  ----------------------------------------
  sudo                               /etc/pam.d/sudo
  GDM login and GNOME lock screen    /etc/pam.d/gdm-password
  SDDM login                         /etc/pam.d/sddm
  Plasma Login Manager               /etc/pam.d/plasmalogin (see below)
  KDE Plasma lock screen             /etc/pam.d/kde

Plasma Login Manager ships its configuration in /usr/lib/pam.d. Copy it to
/etc/pam.d, which takes precedence, and edit the copy:

    sudo cp /usr/lib/pam.d/plasmalogin /etc/pam.d/plasmalogin

Example, /etc/pam.d/sudo:

    #%PAM-1.0
    auth       sufficient   pam_howdy.so
    auth       include      system-auth
    account    include      system-auth
    ...

If you previously installed Howdy by hand, replace any
/usr/local/lib64/security/pam_howdy.so path with plain pam_howdy.so and
remove the manual installation.


4. SELinux
----------

On SELinux systems the howdy-selinux package is installed automatically.
It lets the login screen and the GDM unlock prompt (domain xdm_t) use the
camera. Check that it is loaded:

    sudo semodule -l | grep howdy

If face authentication works with sudo but not at the login or lock
screen, look for denials:

    sudo ausearch -m avc -ts recent


5. Limitations of this package
------------------------------

- Only the default recorder, recording_plugin = opencv, is supported. The
  ffmpeg and pyv4l2 recorders and the "hotkey" rubberstamp need Python
  modules that Fedora does not ship. Do NOT install them with pip: modules
  installed with pip into /usr/local are not seen when PAM runs Howdy, and
  they can conflict with packaged ones.
- The howdy-gtk interface is not packaged. Howdy works without it.
- Rubberstamps (disabled by default): unlike upstream, a stamp that fails
  with an internal error rejects the login, whatever its failsafe or
  faildeadly mode; an empty or invalid rule also rejects the login. Those
  modes only decide what a timeout does: `failsafe` rejects the login,
  while `faildeadly` accepts it. Use `failsafe` if the gesture is required.
  The "nod" stamp also works with use_cnn = true in this package.
- Logging in by face does not give your password to GNOME Keyring or
  KWallet, so they may ask for it after login.


6. Uninstalling
---------------

Remove the pam_howdy.so lines from every file you edited BEFORE removing
the package.
